insert into incoming_cards
  (id, objective_id, lesson_slug, position, question, explanation, visual_description)
values
  ('s1-airplane-parts.primary-controls.c1', 's1-airplane-parts.primary-controls', 's1-airplane-parts', 1, 'The rudder moves the airplane about which axis?', 'The rudder moves the nose left and right about the vertical axis, which is yaw. It does steer the nosewheel on many trainers as well, but that is a separate job done by the same pedals. Pairing each control with its axis early makes everything said about turns and stability afterwards easier to follow. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'An airplane drawn from above with a vertical rod through its centre of gravity and the nose swinging either side of the flight path. The rudder is shaded, the other control surfaces drawn plain.'),
  ('s1-airplane-parts.primary-controls.c2', 's1-airplane-parts.primary-controls', 's1-airplane-parts', 2, 'You roll into a turn using the ailerons alone. What tends to happen to the nose?', 'Ailerons work in opposite directions, and the one that goes down produces more lift and more drag on that wing — which pulls the nose the other way. That is **adverse yaw**, and it is why the rudder goes in with the aileron rather than being an afterthought. Your instructor will show you what coordinated feels like long before it is something you can describe. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the Airplane Flying Handbook.', 'A plan view of an airplane entering a left turn with the right aileron down, extra drag drawn as a short arrow on the right wing, and the nose shown swinging slightly right of the intended path.'),
  ('s1-airplane-parts.primary-controls.c3', 's1-airplane-parts.primary-controls', 's1-airplane-parts', 3, 'When you pull back on the control, what does the elevator change directly?', 'The elevator changes pitch, and pitch changes the angle at which the wing meets the air. Everything else — a change in airspeed, a climb, a descent — follows from that and from what the power is doing. Seeing the elevator as an angle-of-attack control rather than an up-and-down lever is what makes stalls make sense later. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A side view with the elevator deflected and the chord line rotating relative to the oncoming air, with the angle between them shaded. The altitude of the airplane is deliberately unchanged in the frame.'),
  ('s1-airplane-parts.flaps.c1', 's1-airplane-parts.flaps', 's1-airplane-parts', 1, 'What do flaps do for you on an approach?', 'Flaps change the shape of the wing so it produces more lift at a given speed, and they add drag. On an approach that combination is exactly what you want: come down more steeply without speeding up, with a lower nose attitude and a better view of where you are going. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the Airplane Flying Handbook.', 'Two approach paths to the same runway drawn side by side, one shallow with flaps up and one steeper with flaps down, with the nose attitude sketched on each and the pilot''s view over the cowling shown beneath.'),
  ('s1-airplane-parts.flaps.c2', 's1-airplane-parts.flaps', 's1-airplane-parts', 2, 'What limits when you may extend the flaps?', 'Every airplane has a maximum speed at which the flaps may be extended, and exceeding it risks damaging them or their mechanism — which is why the airspeed indicator has a marked range for flap operation. The figure belongs to your airplane rather than to airplanes in general: `[CFI: confirm value]`. Confirm in the POH or AFM for your airplane and the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'An airspeed indicator with the flap operating range marked as an arc, and beside it a wing with flaps partially extended. No numbers printed on the dial face.'),
  ('s1-airplane-parts.flaps.c3', 's1-airplane-parts.flaps', 's1-airplane-parts', 3, 'You go around with full flaps extended. Why are the flaps retracted in stages rather than all at once?', 'With full flaps you are flying slowly and leaning on the lift they provide. Taking all of it away at once, close to the ground and before the airplane has accelerated, is how a go-around turns into a sink. The drag they add is real too, which is why they do come up — in stages, on the schedule in your airplane''s handbook. Confirm in the Airplane Flying Handbook and the POH or AFM for your airplane.', 'A climbing path away from a runway with three marks along it, each showing a smaller flap setting than the last, and the airspeed sketched as increasing between them.'),
  ('s1-airplane-parts.major-components.c1', 's1-airplane-parts.major-components', 's1-airplane-parts', 1, 'What is the tail section of the airplane called?', 'The empennage is the tail assembly. The fuselage is the body, the cowling is the covering around the engine, and a nacelle is a housing that on other aircraft holds an engine. These words earn their keep the first time you have to tell a mechanic which part of the airplane you are talking about. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A trainer drawn in side view with four leader lines out to labels: fuselage, wing, empennage, cowling. The empennage label is drawn no more prominently than the others.'),
  ('s1-airplane-parts.major-components.c2', 's1-airplane-parts.major-components', 's1-airplane-parts', 2, 'Which surfaces make up the empennage on a typical trainer?', 'The empennage carries the fixed stabilizers and the moving surfaces attached to them — elevator on the horizontal, rudder on the vertical. Ailerons and flaps live on the wings. Some trainers have a stabilator, a single moving horizontal surface, instead of a separate stabilizer and elevator; your airplane''s handbook says which you have. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the POH or AFM for your airplane.', 'A rear three-quarter view of the tail with each surface labelled and the moving parts shaded differently from the fixed ones.'),
  ('s1-airplane-parts.major-components.c3', 's1-airplane-parts.major-components', 's1-airplane-parts', 3, 'On your walkaround you find damage. Why does it matter that you can name the part?', '"There is a dent on the leading edge of the left wing, outboard of the strut" gets a useful answer. "Something looks wrong near the front" does not, particularly over the phone. The vocabulary is not for the exam; it is so that what you noticed survives being handed to someone else. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'Two speech bubbles beside the same airplane: one vague and gesturing, one naming the part and its position, with the second bubble drawn reaching a mechanic who is already looking at the right spot.'),
  ('s1-airport-ramp.runway-numbering.c1', 's1-airport-ramp.runway-numbering', 's1-airport-ramp', 1, 'You are lined up on Runway 27. Roughly which way are you pointing?', 'Runway numbers come from the runway''s magnetic heading, rounded to the nearest ten degrees, with the final zero dropped — so Runway 27 points near 270 degrees magnetic, which is west. That is why the numbers mean the same thing at every airport, and why they are worth reading rather than memorising per field. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual and the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A compass rose with a runway drawn across it, the threshold numbers at each end and the corresponding headings marked around the rose.'),
  ('s1-airport-ramp.runway-numbering.c2', 's1-airport-ramp.runway-numbering', 's1-airport-ramp', 2, 'The runway you landed on is Runway 9. What is the number at the opposite end?', 'The same strip of pavement serves two opposite directions, so its two numbers differ by the equivalent of 180 degrees — Runway 9 at one end, Runway 27 at the other. Which one is in use depends on the wind, and it changes during the day. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual.', 'A single runway drawn from above with both thresholds numbered, and a wind arrow beside it drawn first one way and then the other, with the in-use end changing between the two frames.'),
  ('s1-airport-ramp.runway-numbering.c3', 's1-airport-ramp.runway-numbering', 's1-airport-ramp', 3, 'An airport renumbers its runways. What most likely happened?', 'Magnetic north moves over time, and when the drift is enough that a runway''s number no longer matches its magnetic heading, the numbers are repainted and the charts updated. It is a slow business, and it is a good reason to fly with current charts rather than the ones in the back of the airplane. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual.', 'The same runway drawn twice, decades apart, with the magnetic north arrow shifted slightly between them and the painted number changed accordingly.'),
  ('s1-airport-ramp.markings-and-signs.c1', 's1-airport-ramp.markings-and-signs', 's1-airport-ramp', 1, 'You are taxiing and see a red sign with white lettering. What does the colour tell you?', 'Red with white lettering means mandatory: an entrance to a runway or an area you may not enter without permission. Signs that tell you where you are and where things lead use yellow and black instead. Learning the colours before the words means you get the meaning at a glance, which is what you need while taxiing. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual.', 'Three signs drawn side by side at the same size — red with white text, yellow with black text, black with yellow text — with their categories labelled beneath rather than their specific wording.'),
  ('s1-airport-ramp.markings-and-signs.c2', 's1-airport-ramp.markings-and-signs', 's1-airport-ramp', 2, 'Across the taxiway ahead are four yellow lines — two solid, two dashed. What are they?', 'That pattern marks the holding position for a runway. Approaching from the solid side, you stop before the lines unless you have been cleared past them; from the dashed side you are leaving the runway environment and may cross. **Solid means stop** is the version worth carrying in your head at a hold short line. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual.', 'The marking drawn across a taxiway with an airplane approaching from the solid side and stopping, and a second airplane crossing from the dashed side, both drawn in the same frame.'),
  ('s1-airport-ramp.markings-and-signs.c3', 's1-airport-ramp.markings-and-signs', 's1-airport-ramp', 3, 'You are unsure of your position while taxiing at an unfamiliar airport. What is the right thing to do?', 'Stopping and saying you are unsure is a normal request and controllers handle it every day; taxiing on while hoping to recognise something is how aircraft end up where they should not be. An airport diagram, out and in your hand before you start moving, is what keeps this rare. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual.', 'An airplane stationary on a taxiway with a question bubble, and an airport diagram drawn on the pilot''s lap with the position unmarked. Another aircraft continues ahead into an unmarked area.'),
  ('s1-airport-ramp.hold-short.c1', 's1-airport-ramp.hold-short', 's1-airport-ramp', 1, 'You are told to hold short of a runway. What exactly are you being told to do?', 'Hold short means stop on the near side of the marking and stay there until you are told otherwise. Not slow down, not edge forward, not judge it yourself from what you can see. The runway may be being used by an aircraft you cannot see, or by one on a long final you have not spotted. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual.', 'An airplane stopped short of the marking with its nose clear of the line, and a shaded area beyond it labelled as the protected runway environment.'),
  ('s1-airport-ramp.hold-short.c2', 's1-airport-ramp.hold-short', 's1-airport-ramp', 2, 'The runway looks completely empty. Why is crossing without a clearance still wrong?', 'Looking is necessary and is not sufficient. The view from a cockpit on the ground is partly blocked by your own airplane, an aircraft on final can be hard to pick out against terrain, and a runway incursion is one of the most serious things that happens on an airport surface. The clearance is what coordinates everyone; your eyes are the backup, not the authority. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual.', 'A plan view of the airport showing an aircraft on a long final and a vehicle on a crossing taxiway, with a dotted cone marking what the stopped pilot can actually see — neither of them inside it.'),
  ('s1-airport-ramp.hold-short.c3', 's1-airport-ramp.hold-short', 's1-airport-ramp', 3, 'There is no tower at this airport, so nobody can clear you onto the runway. What do the holding position markings ask of you?', 'No tower means nobody is holding the pieces together but you. The marking still marks the boundary of the runway environment, and stopping at it to look down the approach path and announce is exactly the habit that keeps a nontowered field safe. Nothing about an empty frequency proves an empty runway. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual.', 'A nontowered field with an airplane stopped at the holding position, head turned toward the final approach path, and the sky in that direction drawn with an aircraft small and distant in it.'),
  ('s1-airspace-intro.airspace-classes.c1', 's1-airspace-intro.airspace-classes', 's1-airspace-intro', 1, 'What do you need before you may enter Class B airspace?', 'Class B is the one place where talking is not enough: you need an actual clearance into the airspace, and hearing your call sign answered is not the same as being cleared in. Equipment requirements apply too, but they do not substitute for the clearance. If you have not heard the words, you are not cleared. Confirm in 14 CFR and the Aeronautical Information Manual.', 'The layered shape of Class B drawn in profile, with an airplane holding outside the boundary and a speech bubble from the ground containing the word "cleared". The airplane''s path only crosses the boundary after the bubble.'),
  ('s1-airspace-intro.airspace-classes.c2', 's1-airspace-intro.airspace-classes', 's1-airspace-intro', 2, 'The tower is open at a Class D airport you are flying to. What has to happen before you enter its airspace?', 'Class D asks for two-way radio communication to be established, which happens when the controller answers you using your call sign. A reply of "aircraft calling, stand by" is not it. That difference between **established communication** and **a clearance** is worth getting straight early, because it is the difference between Class D and Class B. Confirm in 14 CFR and the Aeronautical Information Manual.', 'Two speech bubbles side by side: one reading a call sign spoken back by the tower, marked as communication established; one reading "aircraft calling, stand by", marked as not. The airspace boundary is drawn beneath both.'),
  ('s1-airspace-intro.airspace-classes.c3', 's1-airspace-intro.airspace-classes', 's1-airspace-intro', 3, 'What decides which class of airspace surrounds a particular airport?', 'Airspace classes describe how much air traffic service is provided and what is being protected — busy airline airports at the top, quiet fields with no tower at the bottom. Once you see the classes as levels of service rather than as arbitrary letters, what each one asks of you starts to make sense. The dimensions of each class are worth learning from the chart and the regulation: `[CFI: confirm value]`. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the Aeronautical Information Manual.', 'A stepped diagram from a quiet grass strip up to a major airport, with the level of service drawn as increasing detail — no controller, a tower, radar — rather than as letters alone. Letters appear alongside, not as the organising idea.'),
  ('s1-airspace-intro.vfr-minimums.c1', 's1-airspace-intro.vfr-minimums', 's1-airspace-intro', 1, 'Why are the VFR visibility and cloud clearance requirements different in different places?', 'The requirements exist so you can see and be seen in time to do something about it. Where traffic is separated for you and where everyone is fast and close to cloud, the numbers change accordingly — which is why they are tied to airspace class and altitude rather than being one figure to memorise. The actual figures are worth learning as a set: `[CFI: confirm value]`. Confirm in 14 CFR and the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A single airplane drawn three times at different altitudes beside a cloud, with the distance it must keep drawn as a shaded standoff that changes size. No figures on the shading.'),
  ('s1-airspace-intro.vfr-minimums.c2', 's1-airspace-intro.vfr-minimums', 's1-airspace-intro', 2, 'The regulation would permit the flight in today''s visibility. Your instructor''s endorsement sets a higher figure. Which one governs?', 'The regulation is a floor, not a target, and the limits written on your endorsement sit above it. Later, as a certificated pilot, you will set personal minimums of your own for the same reason: the regulation describes the worst conditions in which flight is permitted, not the conditions in which you are at your best. Confirm in 14 CFR and with your CFI.', 'Three horizontal lines stacked: the regulation at the bottom, the endorsement above it, today''s weather drawn as a band that sits between them. The aircraft symbol rests on the endorsement line, not the lowest one.'),
  ('s1-airspace-intro.vfr-minimums.c3', 's1-airspace-intro.vfr-minimums', 's1-airspace-intro', 3, 'En route, the visibility ahead is worsening and is heading toward your limit. What is the right move?', 'Deteriorating visibility takes away your options faster than it takes away your altitude, and every one of the other answers is a step further into a situation that is already closing. Deciding early — turning back, landing, or not going at all — is what keeps this a story about a changed plan. Continuing VFR into deteriorating weather is one of the most studied ways pilots come to harm. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the Aeronautical Information Manual.', 'A route drawn left to right with the air ahead progressively greyed out. Two paths branch early: one turning back into clear air, one continuing into the grey and narrowing to nothing. The branch point is drawn well before the grey begins.'),
  ('s1-airspace-intro.sectional-boundaries.c1', 's1-airspace-intro.sectional-boundaries', 's1-airspace-intro', 1, 'On a sectional chart, a dashed blue line encircles an airport. What does it mark?', 'Sectional charts mark each class of airspace with its own line style and colour, and the dashed blue ring is Class D. Learning the symbols as a set — and checking the chart''s own legend rather than trusting memory — is how you tell at a glance whose airspace you are about to enter. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual and the chart legend.', 'A small patch of sectional chart drawn plainly, with one airport ringed by a dashed blue line and the chart legend beside it, the matching legend entry circled.'),
  ('s1-airspace-intro.sectional-boundaries.c2', 's1-airspace-intro.sectional-boundaries', 's1-airspace-intro', 2, 'Inside a segment of controlled airspace, the chart prints one number above another. What are they telling you?', 'The stacked figures give the ceiling and the floor of that segment, so you can tell whether your planned altitude puts you inside it or underneath it. Airspace is three-dimensional, and flying beneath a shelf rather than through it is a normal, legitimate way to route around it. The units and conventions are on the chart legend: `[CFI: confirm value]`. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual and the chart legend.', 'A profile view beside a plan view of the same airspace shelf, with an airplane routed underneath the shelf in the profile and its position shown inside the ring in the plan view — the same flight, legal in one reading and confusing in the other.'),
  ('s1-airspace-intro.sectional-boundaries.c3', 's1-airspace-intro.sectional-boundaries', 's1-airspace-intro', 3, 'You have checked the airspace classes along your route. What else on the chart still needs looking at?', 'Special use airspace sits on the chart in its own symbols and is not described by the class letters, and temporary flight restrictions do not appear on a printed chart at all — they come from your preflight briefing. Both can turn a perfectly planned route into a call from the FAA. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual and your preflight weather briefing.', 'A chart segment showing a hatched special use area crossing a planned route line, and beside it a separate briefing sheet with a temporary restriction drawn on it — deliberately not on the chart, to make the point that the chart alone is not the whole picture.'),
  ('s1-axes-stability.three-axes.c1', 's1-axes-stability.three-axes', 's1-axes-stability', 1, 'The airplane rolls. Which axis is it moving about?', 'Roll happens about the longitudinal axis, the line from nose to tail. Pitch is about the lateral axis, wingtip to wingtip, and yaw is about the vertical axis. All three pass through the airplane''s centre of gravity, which is why loading it differently changes how it behaves. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'An airplane drawn with three rods through its centre of gravity in three different shades, and a small curved arrow around the nose-to-tail rod only.'),
  ('s1-axes-stability.three-axes.c2', 's1-axes-stability.three-axes', 's1-axes-stability', 2, 'Where do the three axes intersect?', 'All three pass through the centre of gravity, and the airplane rotates about that point — which is why where the weight sits changes how the airplane handles. Load it outside the approved range and the behaviour you have been taught to expect stops being the behaviour you get. The limits are in your airplane''s handbook: `[CFI: confirm value]`. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the POH or AFM for your airplane.', 'A side and plan view sharing one marked centre-of-gravity point, with the three axes drawn through it. A second faint outline shows the same airplane loaded differently, with the point shifted.'),
  ('s1-axes-stability.three-axes.c3', 's1-axes-stability.three-axes', 's1-axes-stability', 3, 'In a normal turn, how many axes is the airplane moving about?', 'A turn is not a single-axis manoeuvre. You roll into it, the nose yaws as the turn develops, and back pressure holds the altitude because the lift that was carrying the weight is now partly pulling you round the turn. Seeing the three as things that happen together, rather than as separate lessons, is what makes a coordinated turn make sense. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the Airplane Flying Handbook.', 'A turn drawn in three small panels — entry, established, rollout — with the axes involved highlighted in each, more than one lit in every panel.'),
  ('s1-axes-stability.stability-vs-control.c1', 's1-axes-stability.stability-vs-control', 's1-axes-stability', 1, 'A gust displaces the nose and the airplane returns toward where it was without your input. What is that?', 'Stability is what the airplane does on its own after it is disturbed. A stable airplane heads back toward where it was, which is restful on a long flight and forgiving of a student''s attention wandering. Controllability is a different question: how readily it does what you ask. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A ball resting in a shallow bowl, nudged and rolling back toward the centre, drawn beside an airplane''s nose displaced by a gust and returning. No control inputs drawn in either.'),
  ('s1-axes-stability.stability-vs-control.c2', 's1-axes-stability.stability-vs-control', 's1-axes-stability', 2, 'Why is a trainer not designed to be as manoeuvrable as an aerobatic airplane?', 'The more strongly an airplane resists being displaced, the more effort it takes to displace it on purpose — so designers choose a point on that scale for the job the airplane does. A trainer sits toward stable, which is why it tends to fly itself straight while you are looking at a chart. That is a design choice in your favour, not a shortcoming. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A single slider between two labelled ends, stable and manoeuvrable, with a trainer marked toward one end and an aerobatic airplane toward the other. No point on the slider is marked as better.'),
  ('s1-axes-stability.stability-vs-control.c3', 's1-axes-stability.stability-vs-control', 's1-axes-stability', 3, 'What does trimming the airplane achieve?', 'Trim takes the steady pressure off your hand so you can fly with your fingertips instead of your forearm, and it makes the airplane''s own stability work for you. It does not lock anything, and an airplane in trim still has to be flown — a change in power or configuration means trimming again. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the Airplane Flying Handbook.', 'Two frames of the same airplane holding the same attitude, one with a hand gripping the control and a pressure arrow, one with the hand resting lightly and no arrow. The trim wheel is shaded in the second.'),
  ('s1-axes-stability.adverse-yaw.c1', 's1-axes-stability.adverse-yaw', 's1-axes-stability', 1, 'How does a pilot correct for adverse yaw?', 'Rudder goes in with the aileron and in the same direction as the turn, which cancels the yaw the ailerons produce. Your instructor will have you feel this rather than calculate it — how much and for how long depends on the airplane and on how quickly you are rolling. Confirm in the Airplane Flying Handbook and the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A plan view of a roll into a left turn with the aileron deflection and the rudder deflection drawn together, and the nose tracking the intended path rather than swinging wide of it.'),
  ('s1-axes-stability.adverse-yaw.c2', 's1-axes-stability.adverse-yaw', 's1-axes-stability', 2, 'Why do many airplanes still show adverse yaw even though designers know about it?', 'Designers use several tricks — moving the up-going and down-going ailerons by different amounts, shaping them so the up-going one adds drag — and these reduce adverse yaw rather than abolishing it. Which features your airplane has, and how much is left over, is a matter for your airplane and your feet. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'Two aileron sections drawn in cross-section, one deflected up further than the other is deflected down, with the drag arrows sketched at each. A small residual yaw arrow remains at the nose.'),
  ('s1-axes-stability.adverse-yaw.c3', 's1-axes-stability.adverse-yaw', 's1-axes-stability', 3, 'When is adverse yaw most noticeable?', 'Slow flight with big control movements is where it shows up most — the rudder is less effective at low speed, and larger aileron deflections produce more of the drag difference that causes the yaw in the first place. That is also the part of the envelope where being sloppy with your feet costs the most. Confirm in the Airplane Flying Handbook.', 'Two panels of the same roll input, one at cruise speed with a small yaw arrow and one in slow flight with a noticeably larger one, drawn at the same scale.'),
  ('s1-engines-fuel.fuel-system-path.c1', 's1-engines-fuel.fuel-system-path', 's1-engines-fuel', 1, 'What is the fuel selector''s job?', 'The selector routes fuel — one tank, the other, sometimes both — and gives you a way to cut it off entirely, which matters in a fire or a fuel leak. Mixture, priming and quantity are all somebody else''s job. Which positions your airplane has, and which it should be in for each phase of flight, comes from its own checklist. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the POH or AFM for your airplane.', 'A simplified fuel path drawn left to right — two tanks, the selector, a strainer, and the engine — with the selector shaded and its positions drawn as a small dial beside it.'),
  ('s1-engines-fuel.fuel-system-path.c2', 's1-engines-fuel.fuel-system-path', 's1-engines-fuel', 2, 'Why does a trainer have several fuel drains rather than one?', 'Water is heavier than fuel and settles at the lowest point it can reach — which is different in each tank and again at the strainer. Sampling one and skipping the others tests only part of the system. How many drains your airplane has and where they are is in its handbook, and your instructor will walk you round them. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the POH or AFM for your airplane.', 'A cutaway of a wing tank and a strainer bowl, each with water drawn as a darker layer settled at its own lowest point, and a sample cup beneath each drain.'),
  ('s1-engines-fuel.fuel-system-path.c3', 's1-engines-fuel.fuel-system-path', 's1-engines-fuel', 3, 'What does "unusable fuel" mean?', 'Part of what is in the tanks cannot be counted on — it sits below the pickup, or cannot be drawn in every attitude. What the handbook calls **usable fuel** is what you plan with, and it is less than the tanks hold. This is also why a gauge reading just above empty is not a quantity you can fly on. Your airplane''s usable fuel is in its handbook: `[CFI: confirm value]`. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the POH or AFM for your airplane.', 'A tank in cross-section with the fuel level drawn, the pickup point marked partway up, and the fuel below it shaded differently and labelled as not available in flight.'),
  ('s1-engines-fuel.magnetos-and-runup.c1', 's1-engines-fuel.magnetos-and-runup', 's1-engines-fuel', 1, 'Why does a piston trainer have two magnetos?', 'Two ignition systems, two spark plugs in each cylinder — one so a failure does not stop the engine, and one because lighting the mixture from two places burns it more completely and gives more power. Both run all the time; neither is a spare sitting idle. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A cylinder drawn with two plugs, each traced back to its own magneto, with two flame fronts meeting in the middle. Beside it, the same cylinder with one path greyed out and the engine still drawn running.'),
  ('s1-engines-fuel.magnetos-and-runup.c2', 's1-engines-fuel.magnetos-and-runup', 's1-engines-fuel', 2, 'During the runup you switch to one magneto and the engine speed drops slightly, then recovers when you switch back. What does that tell you?', 'On one magneto the mixture burns less efficiently, so a small drop is normal and is evidence that the system you selected is doing something. What matters is whether the drop is within the limits for your airplane, and whether the two sides are close to each other — **and no drop at all is its own problem**, because it can mean the magneto was never switched off. The acceptable drop and the allowable difference are aircraft-specific: `[CFI: confirm value]`. Confirm in the POH or AFM for your airplane and the Airplane Flying Handbook.', 'A tachometer drawn three times — both magnetos, left only, right only — with the needle a little lower in the two single-magneto frames and the difference between them marked as the thing to compare.'),
  ('s1-engines-fuel.magnetos-and-runup.c3', 's1-engines-fuel.magnetos-and-runup', 's1-engines-fuel', 3, 'The master switch is off and the key is out. Why is the propeller still treated as dangerous?', 'A magneto generates its own spark and does not need the battery — which is why the engine keeps running when electrical power is lost, and why the switches work by *grounding* the magnetos to stop them. If that grounding path is broken, the ignition is live regardless of the switches. Treat every propeller as capable of turning, always, without exception. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A magneto drawn with its own spark path and no connection to the battery, and the grounding wire shown as the thing that switches it off — drawn with a break in it, and the plug sparking anyway.'),
  ('s1-engines-fuel.verify-fuel.c1', 's1-engines-fuel.verify-fuel', 's1-engines-fuel', 1, 'You have drawn a fuel sample into a clear cup. What are you checking?', 'Three things at once: water, which sits as a separate layer at the bottom and can stop an engine; solid contamination; and whether this is the fuel your airplane is supposed to be burning, which you judge by colour and smell. Fuel grades are dyed different colours precisely so the wrong one is visible. The grade your airplane takes, and the colour it should be, are `[CFI: confirm value]`. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the POH or AFM for your airplane.', 'A clear sample cup held against the light with a distinct lower layer of water and a speck of sediment, beside a second cup of clean fuel — both drawn the same size, with the difference in the contents rather than in the drawing.'),
  ('s1-engines-fuel.verify-fuel.c2', 's1-engines-fuel.verify-fuel', 's1-engines-fuel', 2, 'Someone offers to top your trainer up with a fuel of a different grade from the one it takes. What is the right answer?', 'The fuel an engine is approved for is part of its approved design, and fuelling an airplane with something else — including a fuel meant for turbine engines, which has happened and has killed people — is not a judgment call at the pump. Watch the fuelling if you can, check the truck, and check your sample afterwards. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the POH or AFM for your airplane.', 'A fuel truck placard and an airplane''s fuel filler placard drawn side by side at the same size, with the two labels differing — and the pilot''s hand raised between them.'),
  ('s1-engines-fuel.verify-fuel.c3', 's1-engines-fuel.verify-fuel', 's1-engines-fuel', 3, 'You have worked out that your usable fuel covers the flight exactly. What is missing from that plan?', 'Fuel planning works from usable fuel and a burn rate, and then adds what the regulations require you to land with, plus whatever the day suggests — headwinds that were stronger than forecast, a runway that closes, a hold. Fuel exhaustion accidents are usually not mysteries; they are plans with no margin that then met an ordinary surprise. The required reserve is `[CFI: confirm value]`. Confirm in 14 CFR and the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A horizontal fuel bar divided into three labelled segments — the flight, the reserve, and an unlabelled margin — with an arrow showing the planned flight expanding into the second segment when the wind is stronger than forecast.'),
  ('s1-four-forces.four-forces.c1', 's1-four-forces.four-forces', 's1-four-forces', 1, 'The airplane is in straight and level flight at a constant speed. What is true of the four forces?', 'Steady means nothing is changing: the forces are in balance, in pairs. Lift greater than weight is what starts a climb — it is not what sustains level flight. Weight never goes anywhere; the wing is simply carrying it. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'An airplane in level flight with four arrows of equal, paired length — up and down, forward and back. A second faded frame beside it shows the upward arrow longer and the airplane beginning to climb, labelled as a different situation.'),
  ('s1-four-forces.four-forces.c2', 's1-four-forces.four-forces', 's1-four-forces', 2, 'From steady level flight, thrust becomes greater than drag. What happens next?', 'Unbalance a pair and something changes. More thrust than drag means acceleration — and as speed builds, drag builds with it, until the two are back in balance at a higher speed or the airplane is climbing instead. What actually happens depends on what the pilot does with pitch as well, which is why power and attitude are taught together. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A sequence of three frames with the thrust arrow lengthened in the first, the drag arrow growing across the next two, and a speed scale beneath showing the change settling.'),
  ('s1-four-forces.four-forces.c3', 's1-four-forces.four-forces', 's1-four-forces', 3, 'Which statement about drag is true?', 'Drag has two families: the part that comes with making lift, and the part that comes from shoving an aircraft-shaped object through the air. They behave differently with speed, which is why there is a speed where total drag is least — and why the slow end of the range is not the efficient end it looks like. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'Two curves on a single speed axis, one falling and one rising, with their sum drawn above and a dip marked where the sum is lowest. No values on either axis.'),
  ('s1-four-forces.aoa-and-lift.c1', 's1-four-forces.aoa-and-lift', 's1-four-forces', 1, 'Angle of attack is the angle between which two things?', 'Angle of attack is measured between the wing''s chord line and the relative wind — the air actually arriving at the wing. It is not pitch attitude, which is measured against the horizon, and the two can differ a great deal: an airplane descending steeply can have a low nose and a high angle of attack at the same time. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A wing section with the chord line drawn through it and the relative wind arriving at an angle, the angle between them shaded. Beside it, the horizon drawn separately with the pitch angle marked in a different shade, so the two angles are visibly different quantities.'),
  ('s1-four-forces.aoa-and-lift.c2', 's1-four-forces.aoa-and-lift', 's1-four-forces', 2, 'As the angle of attack increases from small to large, what happens to lift?', 'Increasing the angle of attack increases lift — until the air can no longer follow the curve of the upper surface. Past that **critical angle**, the flow separates, lift drops off, and drag climbs sharply. Everything about stalls hangs on that one turning point. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A single curve of lift against angle of attack, rising to a peak and falling away after it, with the peak marked as the critical angle. Small wing sections along the curve show the airflow attached, then separating past the peak. No numbers on the axes.'),
  ('s1-four-forces.aoa-and-lift.c3', 's1-four-forces.aoa-and-lift', 's1-four-forces', 3, 'You reduce power and slow down while holding your altitude. What must happen to the angle of attack?', 'The wing still has to carry the same weight. Slower air over the wing produces less lift at a given angle, so the angle has to increase to make up the difference — which is why slow flight is flown with the nose noticeably higher. It is also why slowing down while holding altitude walks the wing steadily toward its critical angle. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the Airplane Flying Handbook.', 'Three frames at the same altitude, speed decreasing left to right, with the shaded angle of attack growing in each and the lift arrow staying exactly the same length throughout.'),
  ('s1-four-forces.stall-is-aoa.c1', 's1-four-forces.stall-is-aoa', 's1-four-forces', 1, 'Why is the stall speed in the handbook true only under particular conditions?', 'A published stall speed is the speed at which that airplane, loaded and configured a particular way, reaches its critical angle of attack in a particular manoeuvre. Change the weight, the flap setting, or the load you are pulling, and the speed changes — the angle does not. That is why the angle is the reliable idea and the speed is the shorthand. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the POH or AFM for your airplane.', 'One wing at its critical angle drawn three times, each in a different situation — light, heavy, and in a turn — with a different airspeed label beneath each and the identical shaded angle in all three.'),
  ('s1-four-forces.stall-is-aoa.c2', 's1-four-forces.stall-is-aoa', 's1-four-forces', 2, 'Past the critical angle of attack, what is happening over the wing?', 'Below the critical angle the air follows the curve of the upper surface. Past it, the flow breaks away into turbulence, the lift the wing was producing falls off, and drag increases sharply. The airplane is still moving through perfectly ordinary air — what changed is the angle at which the wing is meeting it. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'Two wing sections, one with smooth streamlines following the upper surface, one past the critical angle with the flow breaking into eddies behind the separation point. Both drawn at the same airspeed, labelled as such.'),
  ('s1-four-forces.stall-is-aoa.c3', 's1-four-forces.stall-is-aoa', 's1-four-forces', 3, 'Two identical trainers, one loaded much heavier than the other. What differs about their stalls?', 'More weight needs more lift, and at a given angle of attack more lift needs more speed — so the heavier airplane arrives at its critical angle while still going faster. The critical angle belongs to the wing and does not move. This is also why a stall speed quoted by another pilot is worth nothing until you know how their airplane was loaded. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'Two identical airplanes side by side with different weight arrows, each at the same shaded critical angle, with a speed scale beneath showing the heavier one further to the right.'),
  ('s1-imsafe-pave.imsafe-checklist.c1', 's1-imsafe-pave.imsafe-checklist', 's1-imsafe-pave', 1, 'IMSAFE is a checklist you run on what?', 'Everything else you check before a flight is about the airplane or the conditions. IMSAFE is the one that looks at the pilot: **I**llness, **M**edication, **S**tress, **A**lcohol, **F**atigue, **E**motion. You may hear an instructor give the E as Eating — that version comes from other aviation authorities and some training groups rather than the FAA, so it is worth knowing it exists, but Emotion is the one we teach. It is the first real pilot decision of the day, and the only one nobody else can make for you. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A split graphic. On the left, a preflight walkaround silhouette with a clipboard, labelled "everything else you check." On the right, the same pilot standing still with a hand on their own chest, labelled "IMSAFE." Six small tiles beneath the right-hand figure, one per letter, with the word spelled out. No airplane on the right-hand side at all — the absence is the point.'),
  ('s1-imsafe-pave.imsafe-checklist.c2', 's1-imsafe-pave.imsafe-checklist', 's1-imsafe-pave', 2, 'In IMSAFE, what does the S stand for?', 'Stress — money, school, family, a hard week — rides along in the cockpit whether or not you invite it, and it eats the attention you need for flying. Sleep is a genuinely easy one to land on, but sleep belongs to Fatigue, which has its own letter. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'The six IMSAFE letters shown as a vertical column with each expansion written out, and the S row highlighted. Beside the S row, three small icons — a phone with unread messages, a bill, a clock showing a late hour — drawn small and ordinary rather than dramatic. Beside the F row, a separate icon of a bed, drawn to make the Sleep-versus-Stress distinction visible at a glance.'),
  ('s1-imsafe-pave.imsafe-checklist.c3', 's1-imsafe-pave.imsafe-checklist', 's1-imsafe-pave', 3, 'You wake up with a heavy head cold on the morning of a lesson. What does IMSAFE ask you to weigh?', 'A cold is exactly what the I is for — blocked sinuses and ears hurt on the way down, and feeling rough dulls the judgment you are there to practise. Reaching for medication does not clear the checklist either; that is the M, and many over-the-counter remedies carry their own wait before flight — `[CFI: confirm value]`. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A simple head-in-profile diagram showing the sinus cavities and the middle ear, with small pressure arrows on a descending flight path beside it. Underneath, two checklist rows side by side — Illness and Medication — with a line drawn between them and the caption "treating one lands you in the other."'),
  ('s1-imsafe-pave.pave-checklist.c1', 's1-imsafe-pave.pave-checklist', 's1-imsafe-pave', 1, 'PAVE sorts the risks of a flight into four areas. Which four?', 'PAVE gives you four places to look so that a risk does not go unnoticed simply because you were thinking about something else. Pilot is you, Aircraft is the machine, Environment is the weather, terrain, airport and time of day, and External pressures are the reasons you feel you have to go. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'Four quadrants of equal size, each labelled with its word and carrying one plain icon: a pilot''s head and shoulders; a small trainer aircraft; a cloud over a ridgeline with a moon; and a phone showing a waiting message. Equal weight given to all four quadrants, with no arrows or ranking between them.'),
  ('s1-imsafe-pave.pave-checklist.c2', 's1-imsafe-pave.pave-checklist', 's1-imsafe-pave', 2, 'You told friends you would be on the ground by a certain time, and they are waiting for you there. Where does that belong in PAVE?', 'A promise you have made to somebody else is a reason to keep going when you should turn back, and PAVE gives that its own letter precisely because it is so easy to miss — it is the only one of the four that is not about the flight at all. Recognising it is what stops it deciding for you. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A pilot in the cockpit at the top of the frame with a thought bubble containing a clock and two waiting figures. The four PAVE quadrants sit below, with a dotted line running from the thought bubble past the Pilot quadrant and into External pressures, captioned "it feels like it is about you — it is not."'),
  ('s1-imsafe-pave.pave-checklist.c3', 's1-imsafe-pave.pave-checklist', 's1-imsafe-pave', 3, 'The flight is at night, into an airport with rising ground nearby. Which PAVE area covers the darkness and the terrain?', 'Environment is everything the flight happens in rather than everything you bring to it — weather, terrain, the airport itself, and the time of day. Darkness and rising ground are both squarely in that bucket. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the Aeronautical Information Manual.', 'A night approach drawn in profile: an airplane on a descending path, a ridgeline rising beneath and ahead of it, an airport beacon on the far side of the ridge. Along the bottom, the four PAVE labels with Environment highlighted. The pilot figure is drawn outside the scene, off to one side, to keep the two ideas visually separate.'),
  ('s1-imsafe-pave.hazardous-attitudes.c1', 's1-imsafe-pave.hazardous-attitudes', 's1-imsafe-pave', 1, '"Those rules are for people who do not really know what they are doing." Which hazardous attitude is that?', 'Anti-authority is the attitude that the rules are somebody else''s problem. It is worth separating from Macho, which is about proving something, and from Invulnerability, which is about believing the bad outcome happens to other people. They can appear together in the same sentence, which is why naming them matters. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A regulation page or a placarded limitation shown at an angle, with a hand pushing it aside. Beside it, the five attitude names listed plainly, with Anti-authority marked. No caricature of a pilot — the point is that this is a thought, not a personality.'),
  ('s1-imsafe-pave.hazardous-attitudes.c2', 's1-imsafe-pave.hazardous-attitudes', 's1-imsafe-pave', 2, 'Which thought is the antidote to Invulnerability?', 'Invulnerability is the belief that accidents happen to other people, so its antidote is the sentence that puts you back in the picture. Every option here is a real antidote to one of the five attitudes, which is the point — knowing the list is not the same as matching it. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'Five rows, each pairing an attitude with its antidote, laid out as a simple matching table with the Invulnerability row highlighted. The other four rows stay fully legible rather than greyed out, so the card teaches the whole set even while asking about one.'),
  ('s1-imsafe-pave.hazardous-attitudes.c3', 's1-imsafe-pave.hazardous-attitudes', 's1-imsafe-pave', 3, '"There is nothing I can do about it. Whatever happens, happens." Which hazardous attitude is that?', 'Resignation is the one that does not look like a hazardous attitude, because it sounds like modesty rather than risk-taking. It shows up as a pilot who stops trying to influence the outcome — one who sees an approach going wrong, decides it is out of their hands now, and rides it down instead of going around. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A pair of hands coming off a control yoke, palms open. Beside it, an altimeter or a fuel gauge drawn mid-problem and unattended. Deliberately understated — no alarm colouring — because the card is about an attitude that does not announce itself.'),
  ('s1-pattern.pattern-legs.c1', 's1-pattern.pattern-legs', 's1-pattern', 1, 'You have flown past the end of the runway, turned toward it, and are descending — but you are not lined up with it yet. Which leg are you on?', 'Base is the leg between downwind and final, flown roughly at right angles to the runway, and it is where most of the descent and the last look for traffic happen. Final is the leg aligned with the runway. Getting the names right is not vocabulary for its own sake: on the radio, the leg you say is how everyone else builds a picture of where you are. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual and the Airplane Flying Handbook.', 'A rectangular pattern drawn around a runway with each leg labelled, and a small airplane symbol placed on base. The other legs stay fully drawn and labelled rather than faded, so the whole shape is learned even while one leg is asked about.'),
  ('s1-pattern.pattern-legs.c2', 's1-pattern.pattern-legs', 's1-pattern', 2, 'At an airport with no published exceptions, which way do traffic pattern turns go?', 'Turns in the pattern are to the left unless right traffic is published for that runway — and where it is, it is shown in the Chart Supplement, on the sectional, and on the airport''s segmented circle. Checking before you arrive is the habit; guessing from the wind is how two aircraft end up circling in opposite directions. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual and 14 CFR.', 'Two patterns drawn side by side around identical runways, one left and one right, with the direction of flight arrowed. Between them, a segmented circle with traffic pattern indicators, drawn as the thing that tells the two apart.'),
  ('s1-pattern.pattern-legs.c3', 's1-pattern.pattern-legs', 's1-pattern', 3, 'You are planning a flight to an airport you have not visited. How do you find out its traffic pattern altitude?', 'Pattern altitude is published per airport, and the Chart Supplement is where you look it up while still on the ground. Typical light-aircraft pattern altitude is often around `[CFI: confirm value]` above field elevation, but airports vary, and matching whoever is already there assumes they got it right. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual and the Chart Supplement for that airport.', 'A side view showing field elevation, pattern altitude as a dashed line above it, and the gap between them labelled as height above the field rather than above sea level. Beside it, a Chart Supplement entry drawn with the relevant line highlighted and no numbers filled in.'),
  ('s1-pattern.right-of-way.c1', 's1-pattern.right-of-way', 's1-pattern', 1, 'Two aircraft are approaching the same runway to land. One is lower than the other. What do the rules say?', '14 CFR gives the right of way to the lower aircraft when two are approaching to land — and in the same breath forbids using that rule to cut in front of or overtake the aircraft on final. **A radio call is not a right of way**; it tells people where you are. Confirm in 14 CFR and the Aeronautical Information Manual.', 'A side view of a final approach with two airplanes at different heights on the same path, the lower one ahead. A dotted line shows the higher one attempting to descend and slot in ahead, with that path drawn as the thing the rule forbids.'),
  ('s1-pattern.right-of-way.c2', 's1-pattern.right-of-way', 's1-pattern', 2, 'You are converging with another airplane at about the same altitude, and it is off to your right. Neither of you is landing yet. Who gives way?', 'When aircraft of the same category are converging at about the same altitude, the aircraft to the other''s right has the right of way, so the airplane on your right keeps its course and you give way. Head-on is the different case: there, both aircraft alter course to the right. Seeing the other aircraft first is what lets you act early; it does not change who gives way. Confirm in 14 CFR.', 'A plan view of two airplanes converging at a shallow angle, with the right-hand one continuing straight and the left-hand one curving behind it. A separate small panel shows the head-on case with both aircraft turning to their own right.'),
  ('s1-pattern.right-of-way.c3', 's1-pattern.right-of-way', 's1-pattern', 3, 'You are in the pattern in a trainer and converging with a glider. Who has the right of way?', '14 CFR orders the categories so that the aircraft with the least ability to manoeuvre or go around comes first — a balloon, then a glider, then an airship, with airplanes and rotorcraft giving way to all of them. An aircraft in distress has the right of way over everything. The engine that makes you able to go around is exactly why the rule asks you to be the one who does. Confirm in 14 CFR.', 'A simple ordered row: balloon, glider, airship, airplane, drawn at the same size with an arrow beneath running from "gives way least" to "gives way most". An aircraft in distress is drawn set apart at the front of the row.'),
  ('s1-pattern.go-around.c1', 's1-pattern.go-around', 's1-pattern', 1, 'Which of these calls for a go-around?', 'A go-around is the answer to any approach that is not working out, whatever is making it not work — energy, alignment, a runway in doubt, an instruction, or a landing that bounces. The Airplane Flying Handbook treats it as a normal manoeuvre that every pilot practises, not an emergency and not a mark against you. Confirm in the Airplane Flying Handbook.', 'Four small approach sketches in a row — high, fast, off-centreline, and a runway with something still on it — each with the same climbing arrow drawn away from the runway. One response, four causes.'),
  ('s1-pattern.go-around.c2', 's1-pattern.go-around', 's1-pattern', 2, 'You are high and fast on final and telling yourself you can still salvage it. Why does the Airplane Flying Handbook press you to decide early?', 'The go-around itself does not get harder — your margins do. Deciding while you still have height and options is what makes it routine, and pressing on to "save" an approach is how a landing accident starts. Nobody has to approve it, and a go-around does not need reporting. Confirm in the Airplane Flying Handbook.', 'One approach path drawn with three decision points marked along it, and beside each a shrinking bar representing the height and time remaining. No crash imagery — the shrinking bar carries the point.'),
  ('s1-pattern.go-around.c3', 's1-pattern.go-around', 's1-pattern', 3, 'You have decided to go around. What happens first?', 'Power and a climbing attitude come first, and the configuration changes follow in stages as the airplane accelerates — flaps and gear on the schedule for your airplane, which is in its handbook, not in a general rule. Dumping all the flaps at once can cost you the lift you are relying on at low speed. The exact sequence and speeds are aircraft-specific: `[CFI: confirm value]`. Confirm in the Airplane Flying Handbook and the POH or AFM for your airplane.', 'Three frames along a shallow climb away from the runway: throttle forward and nose raised; flaps moving in a first stage; a stabilised climb with the runway offset to one side so it stays in view. Each frame labelled with the action rather than a speed.'),
  ('s1-pitot-static-gyro.instrument-groups.c1', 's1-pitot-static-gyro.instrument-groups', 's1-pitot-static-gyro', 1, 'Which three instruments work from the pitot-static system?', 'The pitot-static three run on air pressure: the airspeed indicator compares ram air against static, while the altimeter and vertical speed indicator use static alone. The other three are gyroscopic. Sorting them this way is what lets you work out, in flight, which instruments a single failure has taken from you and which ones you can still trust. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'The six instruments drawn in their usual arrangement, with the pitot-static three connected by plumbing lines back to a pitot tube and a static port, and the other three left unconnected in the same picture.'),
  ('s1-pitot-static-gyro.instrument-groups.c2', 's1-pitot-static-gyro.instrument-groups', 's1-pitot-static-gyro', 2, 'The vacuum system fails in a trainer whose attitude and heading indicators are vacuum-driven. What happens?', 'A vacuum failure takes the instruments that system spins and leaves the pressure instruments alone. The catch is that a dying gyro does not go blank — it drifts, leans, and keeps presenting a picture that looks plausible, which is why the vacuum gauge and a cross-check against other instruments matter. **Which instruments are vacuum-driven and which are electric differs between airplanes**, including between two of the same model. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the POH or AFM for your airplane.', 'The six-pack with two instruments shaded to show they are affected, and a small vacuum gauge drawn to one side with its needle out of the normal range. The affected instruments still show a picture, drawn slightly askew rather than blank.'),
  ('s1-pitot-static-gyro.instrument-groups.c3', 's1-pitot-static-gyro.instrument-groups', 's1-pitot-static-gyro', 3, 'Which instrument keeps giving you useful information with no electrical power, no vacuum, and a blocked pitot-static system?', 'The magnetic compass needs nothing but the earth''s magnetic field. It is also awkward — it swings in turns, leads and lags depending on which way you are heading, and settles only in steady flight — but it is the one direction reference that no system failure can take away from you. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A panel drawn with every instrument greyed out except the compass at the top of the windscreen, which is drawn normally. No dramatic lighting — just the contrast.'),
  ('s1-pitot-static-gyro.blocked-pitot-static.c1', 's1-pitot-static-gyro.blocked-pitot-static', 's1-pitot-static-gyro', 1, 'The pitot tube''s ram air inlet becomes blocked while the drain hole stays open. What does the airspeed indicator do?', 'With the ram inlet blocked and the drain still open, the pressure the airspeed indicator depends on leaks away and the needle drops toward zero — while the airplane carries on flying at exactly the speed it was. What the instrument does next depends on whether the drain is blocked too, which is why this card asks about one case only. The response is the same either way: fly the attitude and the power setting you know, and do not chase the needle. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A pitot tube in cross-section with the front inlet iced over and the drain hole open, an arrow showing pressure escaping, and the airspeed needle drawn falling while the airplane beside it flies level and unchanged.'),
  ('s1-pitot-static-gyro.blocked-pitot-static.c2', 's1-pitot-static-gyro.blocked-pitot-static', 's1-pitot-static-gyro', 2, 'The static port becomes blocked in the climb. What does the altimeter do?', 'The altimeter measures the static pressure it is fed, so if that pressure is sealed in, the reading stops moving — and keeps showing an altitude you left behind. The vertical speed indicator goes quiet for the same reason. An altimeter that will not move while the airplane is climbing is the clue. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A climbing airplane drawn at three heights with the altimeter beside it showing the same reading in all three, and the static port marked as sealed.'),
  ('s1-pitot-static-gyro.blocked-pitot-static.c3', 's1-pitot-static-gyro.blocked-pitot-static', 's1-pitot-static-gyro', 3, 'You suspect the static system is blocked. What does your airplane give you to deal with it?', 'Many airplanes have an alternate static source, often drawing from inside the cabin, which restores the static instruments with a small error because cabin pressure is not quite the same as outside. Your checklist says whether you have one, how to select it, and what correction to expect: `[CFI: confirm value]`. Pitot heat is a different thing, and it heats the pitot tube rather than the static port. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the POH or AFM for your airplane.', 'A cockpit panel with the alternate static source control called out, and a small inset comparing the altimeter reading on the normal and alternate sources with the difference marked but not numbered.'),
  ('s1-pitot-static-gyro.six-pack-readings.c1', 's1-pitot-static-gyro.six-pack-readings', 's1-pitot-static-gyro', 1, 'What does the vertical speed indicator tell you?', 'The vertical speed indicator reports a rate of change, not a position, and it lags: it needs a moment after a pitch change before its reading means anything. That is why it is used to confirm a trend rather than to chase, and why a student who flies the vertical speed indicator ends up oscillating up and down through their altitude. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A pitch change drawn on a timeline with the altimeter starting to move immediately and the vertical speed needle catching up a moment later, the delay marked between the two traces.'),
  ('s1-pitot-static-gyro.six-pack-readings.c2', 's1-pitot-static-gyro.six-pack-readings', 's1-pitot-static-gyro', 2, 'What does the turn coordinator show you?', 'The little airplane shows how fast your heading is changing, not the precise angle of bank — two different airplanes at the same rate of turn can be banked differently. The ball is a separate thing entirely: it tells you whether your feet and hands agree, and a ball out to one side in a turn is the condition that turns a stall into a spin. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'The instrument drawn twice at the same indicated rate of turn, with two different bank angles sketched beside it, and the ball shown centred in one and displaced in the other.'),
  ('s1-pitot-static-gyro.six-pack-readings.c3', 's1-pitot-static-gyro.six-pack-readings', 's1-pitot-static-gyro', 3, 'Why does the heading indicator need to be reset against the magnetic compass during a flight?', 'A gyro holds a direction rather than seeking one, and small forces and the earth''s own rotation make it wander. So it is set against the compass — in steady, straight, unaccelerated flight, because that is the only time the compass is telling the truth — and reset at intervals during the flight. How often is a habit your instructor will give you: `[CFI: confirm value]`. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A heading indicator and a magnetic compass side by side at two points in a flight, agreeing in the first and differing slightly in the second, with a small adjustment knob drawn beneath the heading indicator.'),
  ('s1-preflight.airworthiness.c1', 's1-preflight.airworthiness', 's1-preflight', 1, 'What has to be true for an aircraft to be airworthy?', 'Airworthy has two halves, and both must be true: the aircraft matches the design it was certificated to, including any approved changes, **and** it is in condition for safe flight. Flying yesterday tells you about yesterday. The second half is the one you are checking on your walkaround. Confirm in 14 CFR and the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A balance scale with two pans, one labelled "matches its approved design" and one labelled "in condition for safe flight", drawn level. An airplane silhouette sits above the pivot, supported only when both pans are down.'),
  ('s1-preflight.airworthiness.c2', 's1-preflight.airworthiness', 's1-preflight', 2, 'How do you find out whether the inspections the aircraft requires are current?', 'Inspection status lives in the maintenance records, and checking it is part of the pilot in command''s job before flight. The airworthiness certificate itself does not carry an expiry date the way a licence does — it stays valid while the aircraft is maintained as required. Your instructor will show you which records to look at and what to look for. Confirm in 14 CFR and with your CFI.', 'Two open logbooks — airframe and engine — with a hand resting on an entry and a date. Beside them, the airworthiness certificate drawn with no expiry field, so the absence is visible.'),
  ('s1-preflight.airworthiness.c3', 's1-preflight.airworthiness', 's1-preflight', 3, 'On your walkaround you find a piece of equipment that does not work. What decides whether the flight can legally go?', 'Inoperative equipment is not automatically a no-go, and it is not automatically fine either. What matters is whether that item is required — by the regulations, by the aircraft''s equipment list, and by the kind of flight you are making — and whether it has been handled the way the rules require before the aircraft flies. This is a conversation with your instructor and the maintenance staff, not a judgment call at the airplane. Confirm in 14 CFR and with your CFI.', 'A single instrument drawn dark and unlit in an otherwise normal panel. Beside the panel, three small documents fanned out and labelled "regulations", "equipment list", "this flight" — the three things consulted before the airplane moves.'),
  ('s1-preflight.walkaround-flow.c1', 's1-preflight.walkaround-flow', 's1-preflight', 1, 'You have done the walkaround thirty times and know it by heart. Why keep the checklist in your hand?', 'Knowing the flow and using the checklist are not in competition: the flow is how you move around the airplane, and the checklist is how you confirm you missed nothing. The items most often skipped are the ones a pilot is sure they already did. Experienced pilots use checklists more carefully, not less. Confirm in the Airplane Flying Handbook.', 'A pilot mid-walkaround with the checklist held at chest height, and a faint dotted path around the airplane showing the flow. The checklist and the path are drawn as two separate lines that meet at each inspection point.'),
  ('s1-preflight.walkaround-flow.c2', 's1-preflight.walkaround-flow', 's1-preflight', 2, 'Someone stops you to chat in the middle of your preflight. When you get back to it, what is the safest thing to do?', 'An interruption is one of the most reliable ways to lose an item, because you remember the conversation rather than the last thing you touched. Going back to a known point costs a minute. This is the same habit that later keeps interruptions on the ground from following you into the cockpit. Confirm in the Airplane Flying Handbook and the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'The walkaround path drawn as a loop with a break in it where the interruption happened, and an arrow doubling back to the previous inspection point rather than jumping forward.'),
  ('s1-preflight.walkaround-flow.c3', 's1-preflight.walkaround-flow', 's1-preflight', 3, 'The fuel gauges read enough for your flight. What does the preflight ask of you anyway?', 'Fuel gauges in light aircraft are not precision instruments, and pilots have run tanks dry with a needle showing fuel remaining. You look in the tanks, you sample from the drains, and you confirm the fuel is the right grade for your airplane — which is in its handbook, not a general rule. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the POH or AFM for your airplane.', 'Three frames: a fuel gauge needle; a fuel tank opening with a dipstick or visual check; a sample cup held up to the light. The three drawn the same size, so none reads as the shortcut.'),
  ('s1-preflight.start-taxi-runup.c1', 's1-preflight.start-taxi-runup', 's1-preflight', 1, 'What has to happen before you start the engine?', 'A propeller is invisible once it is turning and dangerous before it is. Looking and calling out costs a second and is the one part of the start that protects someone other than you. The rest of the start — throttle setting, mixture, primer — is specific to your airplane and comes from its checklist. Confirm in the Airplane Flying Handbook and the POH or AFM for your airplane.', 'A view from the cockpit looking out over the nose, with the arc the propeller will occupy drawn as a faint circle extending well beyond the spinner, and a person walking on the ramp inside that circle.'),
  ('s1-preflight.start-taxi-runup.c2', 's1-preflight.start-taxi-runup', 's1-preflight', 2, 'The airplane has just started to roll. What do you check straight away?', 'The brake check belongs in the first few feet of movement, where finding a problem means stopping on the ramp rather than discovering it approaching a hold-short line. Radios and engine gauges matter too, and they have their own place on the checklist. Confirm in the Airplane Flying Handbook.', 'A taxiway drawn from above with an airplane just clear of its parking spot, the first short segment of the path shaded and labelled "brakes checked here", and a hold-short line drawn far ahead.'),
  ('s1-preflight.start-taxi-runup.c3', 's1-preflight.start-taxi-runup', 's1-preflight', 3, 'Why is the runup done where it is, before you take the runway?', 'The runup is a test with the airplane still on the ground and still stopped: ignition system, engine instruments, controls free and correct, and whatever else your airplane''s checklist calls for. Warming the engine is part of it, not the point of it. A problem found here is an inconvenience; the same problem found on climbout is not. The settings and the acceptable readings are aircraft-specific: `[CFI: confirm value]`. Confirm in the Airplane Flying Handbook and the POH or AFM for your airplane.', 'An airplane in a runup area beside the hold-short line, with the runway drawn beyond it. A dotted arrow shows the short taxi back to parking as one outcome, and the runway as the other.'),
  ('s1-radio.standard-call.c1', 's1-radio.standard-call', 's1-radio', 1, 'What is the order of a standard radio call?', 'The order is built so the listener can use each piece as it arrives: the facility name tells them the call is for them, your call sign tells them who is speaking, your position tells them where to look, and your request tells them what to do about it. Every call you will ever make — tower, ground, approach, a nontowered field — is that same shape. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual.', 'Four stacked bars of a single call, each labelled with the question it answers: who am I calling, who am I, where am I, what do I want. Beside them, a controller figure with the same four labels arriving in order.'),
  ('s1-radio.standard-call.c2', 's1-radio.standard-call', 's1-radio', 2, 'What should happen before you transmit?', 'Listening first tells you whether the frequency is busy, what the controller is working with, and often what they are about to ask you — and having the call planned in your head is what stops it coming out backwards. Transmitting over someone else means neither call is heard. Having the key words noted down is a normal thing to do while you are learning. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual.', 'A cockpit view with the transmit button and a small thought bubble containing the four parts of the call already assembled. Beside it, a radio waveform showing another aircraft mid-transmission, with the pilot''s hand resting, not pressing.'),
  ('s1-radio.standard-call.c3', 's1-radio.standard-call', 's1-radio', 3, 'Mid-call, the standard phrase you wanted has gone out of your head. What now?', 'Standard phraseology exists because it is compact and unambiguous, and it is worth learning — but being understood is the point of the whole exercise. A controller would far rather hear plain words than a phrase used incorrectly. Everybody sounded rough at the start, including every controller you will talk to. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual.', 'Two speech bubbles of the same size: one dense with standard phraseology, one in plain words, both arriving at a controller who understands both. Neither is drawn as the wrong answer.'),
  ('s1-radio.nontowered-ctaf.c1', 's1-radio.nontowered-ctaf', 's1-radio', 1, 'At a nontowered airport, who clears you to land?', 'There is no clearance at a nontowered field because there is nobody to issue one. Everyone announces on the common frequency, everyone listens, and everyone separates themselves by seeing and being seen. Somebody answering your call is a courtesy and useful information; it is not permission, and nobody there can give you any. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual.', 'A nontowered field drawn with three aircraft in the pattern, each with a speech bubble containing its own position. No control tower in the picture, and no arrows of instruction between aircraft — only eyes drawn looking outward.'),
  ('s1-radio.nontowered-ctaf.c2', 's1-radio.nontowered-ctaf', 's1-radio', 2, 'Self-announce calls at a nontowered field begin and end with the airport''s name. Why the repetition?', 'Common frequencies are shared between airports, sometimes ones quite close together, so a pilot hearing "turning base" needs to know which base, at which field. Saying the name at the front and the back means anyone who tuned in late still knows whether the call concerns them. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual.', 'Two airports drawn some distance apart, both tuned to the same frequency, with one call travelling to both. The call''s first and last words are highlighted, and at the second airport a pilot is shown recognising that the call is not about them.'),
  ('s1-radio.nontowered-ctaf.c3', 's1-radio.nontowered-ctaf', 's1-radio', 3, 'You have announced every leg and nobody else is on the frequency. What can you conclude?', 'A radio is not a traffic detector. Aircraft may legally operate at many nontowered fields without one, a radio can fail, and a pilot can be on the wrong frequency believing they are on the right one. The radio adds to your picture; your eyes are still what keep you apart from other aircraft. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual and 14 CFR.', 'A pattern with your airplane and its speech bubble, and a second aircraft drawn in the same pattern with no bubble at all — present, legal, and silent.'),
  ('s1-radio.readback-errors.c1', 's1-radio.readback-errors', 's1-radio', 1, 'The controller said something and you did not catch it. What do you do?', 'Asking is the shortest way through and costs a few seconds of frequency time. Acknowledging something you did not hear puts a controller under the impression you are about to do a thing you do not know about, which is far more expensive than asking. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual.', 'A broken speech bubble arriving in the cockpit with a gap in the middle, and a short reply going back containing only the request to repeat. The controller''s next bubble is drawn whole.'),
  ('s1-radio.readback-errors.c2', 's1-radio.readback-errors', 's1-radio', 2, 'You think you heard a clearance to cross a runway, but you are not sure it was for you. What do you do?', 'Runway crossings and hold-short instructions are read back for exactly this reason, and an unresolved doubt about one is a reason to stop moving, not to hurry. Controllers work with student pilots constantly; being asked to confirm a crossing is unremarkable to them. A runway incursion is not. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual.', 'An airplane stopped short of a hold-short line with a question bubble going up, and the runway beyond drawn plainly. No collision imagery — the stopped airplane and the unanswered question carry it.'),
  ('s1-radio.readback-errors.c3', 's1-radio.readback-errors', 's1-radio', 3, 'You read back an instruction incorrectly and the controller corrects you. What is the right response?', 'A corrected readback is the system working exactly as designed — that is why readbacks exist. Confirm the correct version so the controller knows you have it, and fly on. An apology takes frequency time that the next aircraft needs, and nobody on the other end is keeping score of how you sounded. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual.', 'Three short exchanges in sequence: a readback with one word wrong, a correction, and a clean readback. The third is drawn no differently from the first two — the sequence is ordinary, not a drama.'),
  ('s1-regs-pic.pic-authority.c1', 's1-regs-pic.pic-authority', 's1-regs-pic', 1, 'Your instructor has endorsed you for solo flight. Today the wind is within every limit written on your endorsement, but stronger than anything you have handled alone. Who decides whether you fly?', '14 CFR makes the pilot in command directly responsible for, and the final authority as to, the operation of the aircraft. An endorsement sets an outer boundary; it does not oblige you to fly to it. **Legal and safe are two different questions**, and only one person answers the second one on the day. Confirm in 14 CFR and the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A single cockpit seat drawn from behind, with three arrows pointing toward it labelled "instructor", "tower", "schedule" — and one arrow pointing outward from the seat to the runway, drawn heavier than the other three.'),
  ('s1-regs-pic.pic-authority.c2', 's1-regs-pic.pic-authority', 's1-regs-pic', 2, 'An in-flight emergency needs immediate action, and the only safe course would break a rule. What do the regulations allow?', '14 CFR allows the pilot in command to deviate from the rules to the extent required to meet an in-flight emergency that demands immediate action, and the Administrator may request a written report afterwards. The authority is real, it is bounded by what the emergency actually requires, and it is accountable after the fact. Confirm in 14 CFR.', 'A rule book drawn open, with a single page lifted aside just far enough for an airplane silhouette to pass through — the rest of the book still closed. A small form icon on the far side, captioned "afterwards, on request."'),
  ('s1-regs-pic.pic-authority.c3', 's1-regs-pic.pic-authority', 's1-regs-pic', 3, 'The maintenance log shows the airplane was signed off this morning, and the school''s dispatcher released it to you. On your walkaround you find something that does not look right. What is your responsibility?', 'Maintenance and the operator have their own responsibilities, but 14 CFR puts the determination that the aircraft is in condition for safe flight on the pilot in command. Nobody else is in the airplane when the decision matters. Saying "it is not going today" is a normal thing for a student to say, and a CFI would rather hear it than not. Confirm in 14 CFR and the Airplane Flying Handbook.', 'Two panels. Left, a logbook page with a signature. Right, a gloved hand at the airplane holding an item at eye level. An arrow runs from left to right and stops short of the airplane, with a small gap drawn deliberately between them.'),
  ('s1-regs-pic.student-limitations.c1', 's1-regs-pic.student-limitations', 's1-regs-pic', 1, 'Your cousin asks to ride along on your next solo flight, just to watch. What do the regulations say?', '14 CFR does not permit a student pilot to carry passengers, and no endorsement or permission changes that — it is a limit on the certificate itself. Solo means solo. The limitation ends when you hold a certificate that allows passengers, which is one of the things the private pilot certificate buys you. Confirm in 14 CFR.', 'A two-seat cockpit drawn from above with the right seat empty and the seatbelt fastened across it, as it would be for solo flight. No crossed-out figure, no scolding — just the empty seat.'),
  ('s1-regs-pic.student-limitations.c2', 's1-regs-pic.student-limitations', 's1-regs-pic', 2, 'A neighbour offers to cover your fuel if you fly a box of parts to an airport an hour away. Can you do it as a student pilot?', '14 CFR bars a student pilot from carrying property for compensation or hire and from acting as pilot in command for compensation or hire. Covering costs still counts as compensation, and "I was going anyway" does not change what the flight is. This is one of the first places where a favour and a commercial operation look the same from the outside. Confirm in 14 CFR.', 'A cardboard box on a passenger seat with a fuel receipt resting on top of it, drawn plainly. Beside it, a small scale, with the box on one side and the receipt on the other, balancing level.'),
  ('s1-regs-pic.student-limitations.c3', 's1-regs-pic.student-limitations', 's1-regs-pic', 3, 'What must be in place before a student pilot flies solo?', 'Solo flight rests on your instructor''s endorsements — for solo in that make and model, kept current, plus the specific endorsements a particular flight needs, such as a solo cross-country. Hours, the knowledge test, and a medical all have their place, but none of them is what authorises you to fly alone. This is also why an AI cannot endorse anyone: **the endorsement is a certificated instructor putting their certificate behind your readiness.** Confirm in 14 CFR and with your CFI.', 'A logbook page drawn open with an endorsement block and a signature, and a date circled. Beside it, a calendar with a currency window shaded, showing that the endorsement has a life span rather than being permanent.'),
  ('s1-regs-pic.required-documents.c1', 's1-regs-pic.required-documents', 's1-regs-pic', 1, 'Which set of documents belongs in the aircraft itself?', 'The aircraft carries its own paperwork: the airworthiness certificate, the registration, the operating limitations (the approved flight manual or placards), and weight and balance information. Maintenance records are kept, but not normally carried in the airplane. Many pilots remember the set with a mnemonic; the point of the card is the contents, not the letters. Confirm in 14 CFR and the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A document pocket in the airplane drawn open, with four labelled edges showing. Separately, on the pilot''s lap, a wallet — the two kept visually apart to make the point that the two sets are different.'),
  ('s1-regs-pic.required-documents.c2', 's1-regs-pic.required-documents', 's1-regs-pic', 2, 'You are walking out to fly solo. What must you have with you personally?', 'For solo flight in an airplane you carry your student pilot certificate, an appropriate medical certificate, and photo identification. Your logbook matters too — it holds the endorsements that make the flight legal — and your instructor will tell you when it must be with you. Confirm in 14 CFR and with your CFI.', 'Three cards laid flat side by side, drawn at the same size and with no personal details filled in. Beneath them, a logbook drawn slightly apart, labelled "where the endorsements live."'),
  ('s1-regs-pic.required-documents.c3', 's1-regs-pic.required-documents', 's1-regs-pic', 3, 'Why is the airworthiness certificate displayed in the cabin rather than filed in a folder?', '14 CFR requires the airworthiness certificate to be displayed so that it is legible to passengers and crew — which is why you see it near the cabin entrance rather than in a folder. It does not need a signature before each flight, and it stays valid while the aircraft is maintained as required. Confirm in 14 CFR.', 'The cabin doorway of a trainer, with the certificate mounted flat and legible at eye height, drawn from the point of view of someone stepping in.'),
  ('s1-solo-knowledge.solo-endorsements.c1', 's1-solo-knowledge.solo-endorsements', 's1-solo-knowledge', 1, 'Before your first solo, your instructor gives you a written knowledge test of their own. What is it for?', 'Before solo, your instructor administers a written test covering the rules that apply to your flight, the airplane''s own procedures and limitations, and the airport and airspace you will be operating in — and they review any wrong answers with you. It is separate from the knowledge test you sit for the certificate, and it is one of the things that has to happen before you fly alone. Confirm in 14 CFR and with your CFI.', 'Two documents drawn side by side and clearly different: a handwritten test sheet with an instructor''s notes on it, and a printed test-centre result form. An arrow from the first points to a logbook endorsement; the second points further down the path.'),
  ('s1-solo-knowledge.solo-endorsements.c2', 's1-solo-knowledge.solo-endorsements', 's1-solo-knowledge', 2, 'Your instructor endorses you for solo flight. What is that endorsement tied to?', 'A solo endorsement covers the make and model you were trained in, and it has to be kept current — it is not a permanent status and it does not transfer to a different airplane on the ramp. Flying a different type solo needs your instructor to train and endorse you for that type too. How often it must be renewed is in the regulation: `[CFI: confirm value]`. Confirm in 14 CFR and with your CFI.', 'A logbook endorsement with the make and model written into it, beside two airplanes on a ramp — one matching the endorsement, one not, drawn at the same size with no crossing-out.'),
  ('s1-solo-knowledge.solo-endorsements.c3', 's1-solo-knowledge.solo-endorsements', 's1-solo-knowledge', 3, 'You want to fly solo to another airport. What does that need beyond your solo endorsement?', 'Solo cross-country is its own step: training in planning and navigating the flight, an endorsement for solo cross-country generally, and your instructor''s review and endorsement of the particular flight you have planned. That last part is deliberate — someone who knows the route, the weather, and you signs off that this specific flight is within what you can handle. Confirm in 14 CFR and with your CFI.', 'A planned route drawn on a chart with an instructor''s pen resting on it, and the logbook beside it showing two separate endorsement blocks rather than one.'),
  ('s1-solo-knowledge.solo-limitations.c1', 's1-solo-knowledge.solo-limitations', 's1-solo-knowledge', 1, 'Your endorsement names conditions — the airport, the weather, the time of day. What is their status?', 'The conditions written on an endorsement are the flight the instructor authorised. Step outside them — a different airport, weather worse than stated — and the endorsement no longer covers what you are doing. Being pilot in command means you can always decide *not* to fly; it does not let you widen the limits you were given. Confirm in 14 CFR and with your CFI.', 'A logbook endorsement with its conditions written out, and beside it a map with a shaded area matching those conditions. A route is drawn staying inside the shading.'),
  ('s1-solo-knowledge.solo-limitations.c2', 's1-solo-knowledge.solo-limitations', 's1-solo-knowledge', 2, 'You are solo in the pattern and the weather is closing in toward the limits on your endorsement. What is the expectation?', 'A limit is not a target to fly up to. Landing early is a completely ordinary outcome, and it is what your instructor is hoping you will do — a student who came back early has made exactly the decision the training is meant to produce. Nobody is disappointed by that; they are disappointed by finding out you pressed on. Confirm in 14 CFR and with your CFI.', 'A pattern with an airplane turning early onto base and landing, with the weather drawn as a band still some distance away rather than overhead.'),
  ('s1-solo-knowledge.solo-limitations.c3', 's1-solo-knowledge.solo-limitations', 's1-solo-knowledge', 3, 'On a solo flight you notice something about the airplane that does not seem right. There is nobody beside you. What do you do?', 'You are pilot in command, which means the decision is yours and the airplane comes back. Ending a flight early over something that turns out to be nothing is a good outcome that costs almost nothing; the alternative is the one that occasionally costs everything. Say what you saw plainly afterwards, even if it is hard to describe. Confirm in 14 CFR and the Airplane Flying Handbook.', 'A single airplane in the pattern with a shortened flight path curving back to the runway, and on the ground a figure talking to an instructor and a mechanic. Nothing dramatic is drawn in the air.'),
  ('s1-solo-knowledge.who-can-endorse.c1', 's1-solo-knowledge.who-can-endorse', 's1-solo-knowledge', 1, 'Who can give you the endorsements you need for solo flight?', 'Endorsements come from an authorized flight instructor — a person holding an instructor certificate, who has given you the training and is willing to put their own certificate behind their judgment about you. Not the office, not a friend who happens to be a pilot, and not an examiner ahead of time. Confirm in 14 CFR.', 'A logbook page with a single endorsement block and a certificate number written into it, and beside it three figures drawn at the same size — an office administrator, a private pilot, an examiner — none of them holding the pen.'),
  ('s1-solo-knowledge.who-can-endorse.c2', 's1-solo-knowledge.who-can-endorse', 's1-solo-knowledge', 2, 'Captain Path says your knowledge of solo requirements is solid. What does that mean for your solo flight?', 'This app can tell you whether you have the knowledge straight. It cannot see you fly, it does not know how you handled a crosswind last week, and it is not permitted to judge readiness to fly alone — that judgment belongs to a certificated instructor who has been in the airplane with you. Anything else would be this product trading your safety for a claim it cannot support. Confirm in 14 CFR and with your CFI.', 'Two panels: a screen with a knowledge checklist fully ticked, and a cockpit with an instructor watching a landing. An arrow runs from the first to the second, not past it.'),
  ('s1-solo-knowledge.who-can-endorse.c3', 's1-solo-knowledge.who-can-endorse', 's1-solo-knowledge', 3, 'You are not certain whether a particular flight is covered by your endorsements. What is the right move?', 'Endorsements are specific, and the person who wrote yours can answer in a sentence what you might otherwise guess at. Asking costs a phone call. Guessing wrong means flying without a valid endorsement, which is a problem for you and for the instructor who signed. Confirm in 14 CFR and with your CFI.', 'A phone and a logbook on a table beside a flight bag, drawn before the walk to the airplane rather than after it.'),
  ('s1-stalls.stall-any-airspeed.c1', 's1-stalls.stall-any-airspeed', 's1-stalls', 1, 'What makes a wing stall?', 'A stall is about the angle between the wing and the air coming at it — the **angle of attack** — not about speed. Past a certain angle, called the **critical angle of attack**, the air stops following the top of the wing and lift falls off. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the Airplane Flying Handbook.', 'Side view of a wing with the relative wind drawn as arrows coming from ahead. A shaded angle between the wing''s chord line and that relative wind, labelled "angle of attack." Three frames: small angle with smooth attached airflow; larger angle, still attached; past the critical angle, with the airflow over the top breaking away into turbulent swirls. The airspeed reading stays identical in all three frames, to make the point that speed did not change anything.'),
  ('s1-stalls.stall-any-airspeed.c2', 's1-stalls.stall-any-airspeed', 's1-stalls', 2, 'You are in a steep turn, well above the speed you normally see a stall happen at. Can the wing stall?', 'Pulling back harder raises the angle of attack, and the wing does not care how fast it is going when it gets there. This is an **accelerated stall**. The speed at which it happens climbs as you pull harder — in a steep turn, by roughly `[CFI: confirm value]`. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the Airplane Flying Handbook.', 'Split screen. Left, an airplane in level flight, wings level, angle-of-attack gauge low, airspeed needle mid-range. Right, the same airplane in a steep banked turn, airspeed needle in the same place, angle-of-attack gauge swung up into a red band. One line of text between them: "Same speed. Different angle."'),
  ('s1-stalls.stall-any-airspeed.c3', 's1-stalls.stall-any-airspeed', 's1-stalls', 3, 'Can a wing stall while the nose is pointed below the horizon?', 'Attitude is where the nose is pointing; angle of attack is how the wing meets the air. They are not the same thing, and a hard pull during a descent or a dive recovery can stall the wing with the nose well below the horizon. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the Airplane Flying Handbook.', 'An airplane in a descent, nose clearly below the horizon line. Two arrows from the wing: one showing where the nose points, one showing the relative wind coming up from below and ahead. The angle between the wing and that relative wind is shaded and large. A caption points out that the horizon tells you attitude, not angle of attack.'),
  ('s1-stalls.stall-warning-signs.c1', 's1-stalls.stall-warning-signs', 's1-stalls', 1, 'Which of these usually shows up as you approach a stall?', 'With less airflow over the control surfaces, the controls go soft and sloppy before the wing quits — often along with a shudder through the airframe, called **buffet**. The engine-roughness option is a different thing entirely: an engine problem is not an aerodynamic stall. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the Airplane Flying Handbook.', 'A short looping animation of a control yoke being moved side to side, with the airplane''s response drawn as a lagging, sluggish wing dip. Beside it, a simple "feel" meter sliding from "crisp" toward "mushy." Faint vibration lines around the airframe to suggest buffet. No numbers on screen.'),
  ('s1-stalls.stall-warning-signs.c2', 's1-stalls.stall-warning-signs', 's1-stalls', 2, 'The stall warning goes off. What does that mean?', 'The stall warning is an early warning, not a report of something that already happened — it triggers before the wing stalls, giving you room to act. How much room varies by aircraft: `[CFI: confirm value]`. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge, and in the POH or AFM for the specific airplane.', 'A single sliding scale, left to right: "flying normally" → "stall warning" → "critical angle of attack" → "stalled." A marker sits on the stall-warning band, with the gap between it and "stalled" shaded and labelled "your margin — varies by airplane." The gap is drawn without a number on it.'),
  ('s1-stalls.stall-warning-signs.c3', 's1-stalls.stall-warning-signs', 's1-stalls', 3, 'The airspeed indicator shows a comfortable number. Does that guarantee the wing is not about to stall?', 'The marked stall range on the dial assumes a particular set of conditions. Load the wing up in a turn or a pull-up and the wing can stall at a higher indicated speed. The instrument is not lying to you — it is answering a different question than the one that matters. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'An airspeed indicator with the needle steady in the green. Over it, a ghosted second marking showing the stall point shifting upward as a small bank-angle dial beside it increases. The needle never moves; the stall point slides up to meet it.'),
  ('s1-stalls.stall-recovery-spins.c1', 's1-stalls.stall-recovery-spins', 's1-stalls', 1, 'What is the first thing you do to recover from a stall?', 'Only reducing the angle of attack makes the wing fly again — everything else comes after. The pull-back-to-stop-the-descent option is the instinct that gets people hurt: pulling harder is exactly what deepens a stall. Power and wings-level follow, in the order your airplane''s procedure gives. Confirm in the Airplane Flying Handbook, and follow the POH or AFM for your airplane.', 'Three panels. First, the wing past its critical angle with separated airflow and a downward arrow. Second, the control column moving forward and the angle of attack visibly shrinking, airflow reattaching. Third, the airplane flying again with the nose coming back up. A caption under panel two: "This is the step that ends the stall."'),
  ('s1-stalls.stall-recovery-spins.c2', 's1-stalls.stall-recovery-spins', 's1-stalls', 2, 'What has to be happening for a stall to develop into a spin?', 'A spin needs two ingredients at once: a stalled wing, and yaw. One wing is more stalled than the other, and the airplane rolls and rotates — this is why coordinated flight matters so much near the stall. Bank angle alone does not cause it. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the Airplane Flying Handbook.', 'Two-ingredient graphic: one icon of a stalled wing, one icon of a yaw arrow around the vertical axis, joined by a plus sign, equalling a small rotating-airplane icon. Beside it, a rudder pedal and a slip-skid ball centred, captioned "keeping the ball centred is what keeps the second ingredient out."'),
  ('s1-stalls.stall-recovery-spins.c3', 's1-stalls.stall-recovery-spins', 's1-stalls', 3, 'Why is a spin most dangerous close to the ground?', 'Recovering costs height, and how much depends on the airplane and on how far the spin has developed: `[CFI: confirm value]`. That is the whole reason the traffic pattern is where stall and spin awareness matters most. Confirm in the Airplane Flying Handbook, and in the POH or AFM for the specific airplane.', 'A side-on altitude ribbon with an airplane entering a spin near the top and the recovery drawn as a shaded band of height consumed before level flight. The same band is then shown overlaid at traffic-pattern height, where it reaches the ground. The band is shaded without a figure printed on it.'),
  ('s1-weather-intro.weather-drivers.c1', 's1-weather-intro.weather-drivers', 's1-weather-intro', 1, 'What starts the chain that ends up as wind?', 'The surface does not heat evenly — land warms faster than water, dark ground faster than light — and that unevenness produces differences in pressure. Air moves from higher pressure toward lower, and the earth''s rotation then bends the path it takes. Rotation shapes the wind; it does not start it. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A cross-section of ground and water under a sun, with warm air drawn rising over the land and cooler air moving in across the water to replace it. The pressure difference labelled between the two columns rather than shown as numbers.'),
  ('s1-weather-intro.weather-drivers.c2', 's1-weather-intro.weather-drivers', 's1-weather-intro', 2, 'The temperature and the dew point are getting closer together through the afternoon. What does that tell you?', 'The dew point is the temperature at which the air can hold no more water vapour. As the temperature falls toward it, the air is closer to giving that moisture up as cloud, mist, or fog — so a narrowing spread is a warning, not something to wait on until the two numbers meet. A useful spread to start paying attention to is `[CFI: confirm value]`. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'Two lines on a simple time axis, temperature falling and dew point roughly level, converging toward the right. Where they close, small cloud and fog symbols appear along the bottom. No values on the axis.'),
  ('s1-weather-intro.weather-drivers.c3', 's1-weather-intro.weather-drivers', 's1-weather-intro', 3, 'You are told the air mass today is unstable. What do you expect to fly in?', 'Unstable air keeps rising once something starts it moving, which produces convection: cumulus clouds that build, showery rather than steady precipitation, turbulence, and often good visibility between the showers. Stable air is the other picture — smoother, hazier, with steady precipitation if any. Knowing which one you are in tells you what kind of day it will be long before you look at any single report. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'Two side-by-side sky scenes: one layered and flat with haze, one with vertical cumulus and clear air between. Each labelled with what the ride is like rather than with a definition.'),
  ('s1-weather-intro.metar-and-taf.c1', 's1-weather-intro.metar-and-taf', 's1-weather-intro', 1, 'What is the difference between a METAR and a TAF?', 'A METAR reports conditions that were observed at a station at a particular time. A TAF is a forecast for a period, covering the area around an airport. Reading the observation tells you what is; reading the forecast tells you what somebody expects — and only one of those can be wrong about the future. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual and the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'Two panels on a timeline: the METAR anchored to a single point marked "now", the TAF drawn as a band stretching to the right across a period, with a dotted boundary showing the area it covers.'),
  ('s1-weather-intro.metar-and-taf.c2', 's1-weather-intro.metar-and-taf', 's1-weather-intro', 2, 'The wind direction in a written METAR and the wind a controller reads to you are referenced differently. How?', 'Written reports give wind direction relative to true north, while the wind spoken to you by a tower or on an ATIS is relative to magnetic north — which is also how runways are numbered. The difference matters most when you are picking a runway from a report you read on the ground. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual.', 'A single wind arrow drawn once, with two compass roses beneath it — one true, one magnetic — and the angle between them marked. A runway numbered in the magnetic frame sits beside them.'),
  ('s1-weather-intro.metar-and-taf.c3', 's1-weather-intro.metar-and-taf', 's1-weather-intro', 3, 'You checked the weather at home this morning and it looked fine. You arrive at the airport three hours later. What now?', 'Observations are replaced through the day and forecasts get amended, so weather you checked hours ago is history rather than information. Looking outside is worth something, but it tells you about here and now, not about where you are going or the hour after next. Confirm in the Aeronautical Information Manual.', 'A clock face with three marks, and beneath each a small weather card. The earliest card is greyed and stamped with the time it was issued; the latest is drawn clearly, with the sky outside the window shown the same in all three.'),
  ('s1-weather-intro.fog-storms-icing.c1', 's1-weather-intro.fog-storms-icing', 's1-weather-intro', 1, 'Clear sky, light wind, moist air, and a long cool night. What are you likely to find at the airport in the morning?', 'On a clear, nearly calm night the ground radiates heat away and cools the air in contact with it. If that air is moist enough to reach its dew point, fog forms — often at its worst around sunrise, which is exactly when an early lesson is scheduled. It usually lifts with sun and wind, but "usually" is not a flight plan. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.', 'A night-to-morning sequence in three frames: clear starry sky with heat drawn leaving the ground; a shallow fog layer forming over the field before dawn; the same field with the layer thinning under early sun. The airplane stays parked in all three.'),
  ('s1-weather-intro.fog-storms-icing.c2', 's1-weather-intro.fog-storms-icing', 's1-weather-intro', 2, 'What three ingredients does a thunderstorm need?', 'Every thunderstorm needs moisture, air that keeps rising once it starts, and a lifting action to start it — heating, terrain, or a front. Knowing the ingredients is how you see one coming in a forecast rather than out of the windscreen. For a light trainer the response is distance, not technique: give storms a wide berth, `[CFI: confirm value]`, and never try to pass beneath one. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the Aeronautical Information Manual.', 'Three labelled ingredient icons — water vapour, an unstable temperature profile, and a lifting mechanism — feeding into a single building storm cell. A separate plan view shows an airplane''s track curving well around the cell rather than under it.'),
  ('s1-weather-intro.fog-storms-icing.c3', 's1-weather-intro.fog-storms-icing', 's1-weather-intro', 3, 'What has to be present for structural ice to form on your airplane?', 'Structural ice needs both visible moisture — cloud, rain, drizzle — and the airplane to be somewhere at or below freezing. Cold clear air will not ice you up, and cloud well above freezing will not either. A typical trainer is not approved for flight into known icing, so the plan is to stay out, and to change altitude or turn back early if you find it. Ice also changes what the wing can do at exactly the moment you need it most. Confirm in the Pilot''s Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge and the POH or AFM for your airplane.', 'A vertical slice of sky with a freezing level drawn across it and cloud straddling it. The portion of cloud at or below freezing is shaded as the icing zone, with an airplane shown turning back and descending out of it.'),
  ('s1-welcome.training-path.c1', 's1-welcome.training-path', 's1-welcome', 1, 'Which of these describes the shape of private pilot training?', 'Ground knowledge and flying grow together: dual lessons, then solo flight once your instructor endorses it, then cross-country work, and two tests — a knowledge test on the ground and a practical test in the airplane. How long each part takes is personal, and comparing your hours to someone else''s tells you very little. The exact requirements depend on whether you train under Part 61 or at a Part 141 school. Confirm in 14 CFR and with your CFI.', 'A path drawn left to right with five labelled stops — dual lessons, first solo, cross-country, knowledge test, practical test — with the spacing between them deliberately uneven and unnumbered.'),
  ('s1-welcome.training-path.c2', 's1-welcome.training-path', 's1-welcome', 2, 'The knowledge test and the practical test — what is the difference?', 'The knowledge test is a written exam you sit at a testing centre. The practical test — the checkride — has two parts: an oral discussion and a flight, both with an examiner. Passing the written is not the finish line; it is one of the things that has to be in place before the checkride. Confirm in 14 CFR and with your CFI.', 'Two panels: a testing-centre desk with a screen, and a cockpit with two seats and an examiner. A single certificate drawn beyond both, reached only by a line passing through each in turn.'),
  ('s1-welcome.training-path.c3', 's1-welcome.training-path', 's1-welcome', 3, 'Another student got their certificate in far fewer hours than you are on track for. What does that tell you?', 'Flying every week builds skill faster than flying once a month; a winter of weather or an airplane in maintenance stretches a timeline; and the minimum hours in the regulations are a floor, not a target most people meet. The standard you are being measured against is fixed and public. The time it takes to reach it is not, and it is not a measure of whether you belong here. Confirm in 14 CFR and with your CFI.', 'Two paths to the same destination drawn side by side, one with evenly spaced steps and one with gaps and doubled-back sections, both arriving at the same point. No figure is drawn as ahead or behind.'),
  ('s1-welcome.what-is-the-acs.c1', 's1-welcome.what-is-the-acs', 's1-welcome', 1, 'What is the Airman Certification Standards document?', 'The ACS sets out, in the open, what an applicant must know, the risks they must be able to manage, and the skills they must demonstrate. It is not a secret held by examiners — you can read the same document they use. Knowing that the standard is public, and the same for everyone, is worth something on the days training feels like guesswork. Confirm in the Airman Certification Standards and with your CFI.', 'A single open document with three labelled columns — knowledge, risk management, skill — and two readers on either side of it, a student and an examiner, both looking at the same page.'),
  ('s1-welcome.what-is-the-acs.c2', 's1-welcome.what-is-the-acs', 's1-welcome', 2, 'Does the standard in the ACS change depending on the student?', 'The standard does not move. What can and should differ is the teaching: the examples used, the pace, the support, the cost of getting to the airport. **Nobody is served by a pilot certificate that meant something different for them than for the person next to them** — least of all the pilot holding it. Confirm in the Airman Certification Standards.', 'A single horizontal line labelled as the standard, with several different routes climbing to it from different starting heights. Every route reaches the same line; none of them stops short.'),
  ('s1-welcome.what-is-the-acs.c3', 's1-welcome.what-is-the-acs', 's1-welcome', 3, 'The ACS gives tolerances — how far from a target altitude or heading you may be and still meet the standard. What are they for?', 'Nobody flies a perfect altitude forever, so the standard says how much variation is acceptable and expects you to notice and correct. Having that written down protects you: it makes passing a matter of meeting a published standard rather than of impressing someone. The tolerances themselves are in the ACS: `[CFI: confirm value]`. Confirm in the Airman Certification Standards.', 'An altitude trace wandering slightly above and below a target line, with a shaded band around the line showing the tolerance and the trace staying inside it — including the corrections back toward the middle.'),
  ('s1-welcome.cfi-vs-ai.c1', 's1-welcome.cfi-vs-ai', 's1-welcome', 1, 'Captain Path has worked through every lesson with you and you can answer anything it asks. Who can endorse you for solo flight?', 'An endorsement is a certificated instructor putting their own certificate behind a judgment that you are ready — made by someone who has flown with you. No amount of ground knowledge substitutes for that, and this app cannot give it, will not claim to, and would be worth less if it pretended otherwise. What it can do is make sure the knowledge part is solid before you get in the airplane. Confirm in 14 CFR and with your CFI.', 'A logbook endorsement block with a pen and a signature, and beside it a screen showing a chat — the screen drawn with no pen and no signature line at all.'),
  ('s1-welcome.cfi-vs-ai.c2', 's1-welcome.cfi-vs-ai', 's1-welcome', 2, 'What is an AI ground instructor genuinely useful for?', 'Ground knowledge is where an AI is genuinely good: it is available at midnight, it does not get tired of a question asked four different ways, and it costs nothing per hour — which matters when instruction is billed by the hour. Flight instruction, endorsements, and any judgment about your readiness belong to a human instructor. Confirm with your CFI.', 'Two columns of equal size, headed "on the ground" and "in the airplane", with a phone in the first and a cockpit with two seats in the second. Neither column is drawn larger than the other.'),
  ('s1-welcome.cfi-vs-ai.c3', 's1-welcome.cfi-vs-ai', 's1-welcome', 3, 'Something Captain Path told you does not match what your CFI says. What do you do?', 'Your instructor is accountable for your training and knows the specifics this app cannot — the aircraft, the field, the local procedures, and what you did last Tuesday. Bringing the disagreement to them is also how errors here get found and fixed. Asking "my ground school said this, why is it different?" is a good question, not a challenge. Confirm with your CFI and the current FAA handbooks.', 'Two speech bubbles meeting at a student figure, one from a screen and one from an instructor, with the student''s next line drawn as a question mark directed at the instructor rather than at either bubble.');
insert into incoming_options (card_id, option_id, text, is_correct)
values
